
To make sure you get acccess to Bastion:
(linux based) OS make it easier,  make sure you have created a public and private key.
ssh-keygen and enter for defaults most likely  would create in ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 and ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub

Update keypair.tf file with the path for your public key.
public_key = file("~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub")

**if above completed, ready to run terraform.**

you are ready to run terraform init the terraform plan and apply.
Code was tested and successfully met the requirements of challenge, well in my opinion.

Terraform apply will prompt you for the IP to allow access to bastion.
you can use 0.0.0.0/0 or your IP x.x.x.x/32
once terraform creates all resources:

ssh ec2-user@<Public IP BASTION ec2>

**Some extra to check httpd config**
You could also access via ssh to your ec2 instances in the ASG, not recommended, but i did for troubleshooting purposes.
cp from your terminal ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 to the bastion and name <file.key> and set chmod 400 to the new file.

then to ssh to private instances:
ssh -i file.key ec2-user@IP

####################
Test Load balancer:
####################
Grab dns info from the new load balancer created. (yours will be different.
example:
internal-testti20261010150500518500000006-1964878781.us-east-1.elb.amazonaws.com
once you ssh to bastion you can test it as follows:

[ec2-user@ip-10-1-1-59 ~]$ wget -qO- http://internal-testti20261010150500518500000006-1964878781.us-east-1.elb.amazonaws.com

Output:
  <body>
    <h1>Amazon Linux 2023</h1>
    <p>Instance private IP: 10.1.2.237</p>
  </body>

if you run the command again you should receive a different IP
  <body>
    <h1>Amazon Linux 2023</h1>
    <p>Instance private IP: 10.1.2.222</p>
  </body>

####Congrats is working ALB routing traffic to ec2 instances which are in ASG. #######



